            <ArticleTitle>Toward the clinical use of circulating biomarkers predictive of bone union.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1125-1133</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/bmm-2017-0180</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Current methods for diagnosis of bone healing after treatment of trauma injuries rely on clinical findings and the use of imaging methodologies that provide conclusive results but only at mid/long-term post-intervention. In this Perspective we present and discuss incipient evidence on biomarkers that may serve for monitoring the progression of bone healing as well as predicting eventual nonunion outcomes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
